## M5 — Failure bookkeeping has no timeout; DB outage stalls the single worker loop

- **Status:** not started
- **Where:** `pipeline/pipeline/ingest/worker.py:1084-1091` (`_handle_job_failure` awaited
  outside any timeout), `pipeline/pipeline/store/db.py:20-21` (`psycopg.connect` with no
  connect timeout)

### Problem

`_handle_job_failure` (→ `_requeue` / `_finish_fail` / `_notify_ingest_terminal`) runs
without a timeout and each call opens fresh connections. `psycopg.connect` has no
connect/statement timeout configured, so during a DB outage the worker can block minutes
per failed job — while holding the claim of a job the reaper (same process, H2) cannot
reclaim. Separately, `_claim_one`'s `except Exception` logs at ERROR every 2s during an
outage (log spam + connection churn).

### Fix

- Add `connect_timeout` (and ideally `options=-c statement_timeout=...`) to
  `psycopg.connect` in `db.py`, or wrap `_handle_job_failure` in
  `asyncio.timeout(30)`.
- Downgrade the claim-error log to WARNING (it is an expected condition, not an
  exception), and log retryable job failures at WARNING instead of `log.exception` (line
  1106) — the traceback belongs in Sentry via `obs.capture_error`, not in the log at
  ERROR for every transient provider 503.

- **Files to touch:** `pipeline/pipeline/store/db.py`, `pipeline/pipeline/ingest/worker.py`.
- **Verify:** stop Postgres → worker keeps polling without long stalls, logs at WARNING;
  restart Postgres → queued jobs resume.

